#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522723 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068701 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e5366dd-a374-5abd-bdf6-77f378f97145" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a11fc5e2-b2d0-5079-ab86-b342ef825f79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a11fc5e2-b2d0-5079-ab86-b342ef825f79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522355} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3a17bfa-7fbe-51f4-8e56-171039043e1e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522661}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "473f318b-f2f7-5bdb-8ff8-b046ab67dc91" :question/math-academy-id "q-132985" :question/problem "Find the value of $f(x)$ at $x=4.$\n\n$f(4)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We substitute $4$ for $x$ in the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3\\sqrt{x}+2 \\\\ f(4) & =3\\sqrt{4}+2 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2+2 \\\\  & =6+2 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059632 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8daba5bf-5162-5779-9512-f22d0c8c0320" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Piecewise Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7286d536-8e39-5508-813e-ce3228d5abaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7286d536-8e39-5508-813e-ce3228d5abaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522247} :answer-field/id #uuid "0af79da6-d74c-55ae-af40-2ee047d59dfe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522561}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0286ada6-f474-5c67-9cad-74d4d70fc3df" :question/math-academy-id "q-132986" :question/problem "$h(4)\\,=\\,${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $x=4$ is in the interval $x\\ge 0,$ we use the second branch of the piecewise function, namely $h(x)=2x+1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(4) & =2(4)+1 \\\\  & =8+1 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522763 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129109 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82d522a8-ae3c-5723-909e-540ddac1d9da" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Roots Given a Function Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76d3d9b6-d262-599b-a5f0-9337f5d37053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522253}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76d3d9b6-d262-599b-a5f0-9337f5d37053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522253} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e94f094-57f9-5f39-b10c-5dbe75ec001e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9fac61ef-0417-5de3-9911-e04b4f578edc" :question/math-academy-id "q-132987" :question/problem "What is the root of the function $g(x)?$\n\nThe root is at $x\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The roots are the solutions to the equation $g(x)=0,$ which we solve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =0 \\\\ 2x-6 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has the single root $x=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522755 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124564 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ec3d139-c43a-5547-b0ae-147e41a55180" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Global Minimum of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f732096a-28a1-5dd2-9a4a-687e42083476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522544}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f732096a-28a1-5dd2-9a4a-687e42083476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522544} :answer-field/id #uuid "d57aee12-66ab-51f7-a521-b364c55461d8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522662}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8db22404-98b1-5e60-b0e3-c4c2f250e097" :question/math-academy-id "q-132988" :question/problem "Using the graph of $y=h(x)$ on the interval $[-3,2],$ find the global maximum value of $h(x)$ on this interval.\n\n${h}_{max}\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n* The global maximum of a function is the highest value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n* A global maximum point of a function is a point $(x,y)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $h(x)=5.$ \n\n![](images/70/70c48ae2f451dfb8194a0f93aaaec406c36d381a8d85d2565320c5a7860351e7.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the global maximum is ${h}_{max}=5.$ The function reaches this value when $x=2,$ so the global maximum point is $(2,5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522719 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054613 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2aafd8db-c723-508f-9ea3-43881f0d9140" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sum or Difference of Two Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eaddaa63-63d1-5782-92be-b40ba9560fc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{x}+2x-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eaddaa63-63d1-5782-92be-b40ba9560fc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{x}+2x-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522521} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d18190d-0dbe-55c8-b80a-4828baa4e2de" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3d43c3ac-d004-5e03-b676-57e7a9141cc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-132989" :question/problem "Write the expression for the function $(f+g)(x)$ in terms of $x.$\n\n$(f+g)(x)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We find $(f+g)(x)$ by adding the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(x) & =(3\\sqrt{x}+2)+(2x-6) \\\\  & =2x+3\\sqrt{x}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522813 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054612 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55d291ea-d722-5852-9c3a-faaba8e6df3e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Quotient of Two Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f249961e-45e9-5421-a45e-02ccbe1f7252" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f249961e-45e9-5421-a45e-02ccbe1f7252" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522535} :answer-field/id #uuid "34781f40-a9b8-5b38-ad7d-e77c41c4ba31" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe533203-bd0f-5d2e-8bef-eff5c39a0f20" :question/math-academy-id "q-132990" :question/problem "Find the value of $(f/h)(4).$\n\n$(f/h)(4)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The notation $(f/h)(x)$ means the quotient of the functions $f(x)$ and $h(x).$ \n\nTo evaluate $(f/h)(4),$ we need to divide $f(4)$ by $h(4).$\n\nEarlier, we found the following values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4) & =8 \\\\ h(4) & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n(f/h)(4)=\\frac{f(4)}{h(4)}=\\frac{8}{9}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522736 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129298 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f954ff1-3f02-5fb1-aca2-5911d5636167" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Composite Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2f02b4d-fe99-5efc-bd3d-db782d48acae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522427}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2f02b4d-fe99-5efc-bd3d-db782d48acae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522427} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e2e9d21-1d9d-5db5-8e76-6f60eb76390f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6c436406-49e2-5628-8484-ed489c154cfd" :question/math-academy-id "q-132991" :question/problem "$(f∘h)(4)\\,=\\,${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The expression $(f∘h)(4)$ is equivalent to $f(h(4)).$\n\nPreviously, we found that \n\n$$\nh(4)=9.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=9$ into $f(x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(h(4)) & =f(9) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{9}+2 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3+2 \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522697 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179650 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ebb0209-904f-598a-9ff2-ab9d4d0cabca" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Range of a Linear Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96993b19-490b-55e2-9b12-6fd2024180dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96993b19-490b-55e2-9b12-6fd2024180dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522328} :answer-field/id #uuid "41b0f793-25d5-544d-9bd8-34056c7e9a16" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522593} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd0c47e2-c7b2-5eaa-8bf3-3fee7f41fd2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd0c47e2-c7b2-5eaa-8bf3-3fee7f41fd2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522443} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1c16e24-0c28-5784-a074-fb5d02807445" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0715be61-dd61-5ecb-88f5-3c63416166d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-132992" :question/problem "Consider the function $g(x)$ for $x\\in [2,4].$ What is its range over the given domain?\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\le \\,g(x)\\,\\le $\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function $g(x)=2x-6.$\n\n![](images/de/de124058817fded27ad4ca9f9537f4a7d09ccd8836c96b0e64580e28dc9ab1ec.png)\n\nThen, we find the lowest and highest $y$-values in the graph over the domain $x\\in [2,4].$\n\n* The lowest $y$ value is $-2.$ This occurs at the point $(2,-2).$\n\n* The highest $y$ value is $2.$ This occurs at the point $(4,2).$\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-2$ and $2,$ including the endpoints.\n\nTherefore, the range is $-2\\le y\\le 2,$ which can also be written as \n\n$$\n-2\\le g(x)\\le 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522749 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083359 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "06e51d62-ad2a-51c3-b276-eeb60022ab48" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Inverse Function Using the Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e01d97ce-e4d8-5d2a-97b7-3ba004fbc57e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e01d97ce-e4d8-5d2a-97b7-3ba004fbc57e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522492} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a439040-17f5-58b3-90c2-a81073227783" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "84ed963d-febb-5737-ad58-631a391c71a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-132993" :question/problem "Suppose that $r(x)=\\frac{1}{2}x+k$ is the inverse of $g(x).$ What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $r$ is the inverse of $g,$ then \n\n$$\nr(g(x))=x.\n$$\n\nTo find $r(g(x)),$ we write down the function $r$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $g(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}x+k \\\\ r(g(x)) & =\\frac{1}{2}g(x)+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $g(x)=2x-6$ into the right-hand side of our expression and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(g(x)) & =\\frac{1}{2}g(x)+k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x-6)+k \\\\  & =x-3+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we know that $r(g(x))=x.$ Equating the expression above with $x$ and solving for $k$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(g(x)) & =x \\\\ x-3+k & =x \\\\ \\cancel{x}-3+k & =\\cancel{x} \\\\ -3+k & =0 \\\\ k & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
